  "overview": "_. There is an Introduction to the series by Bamber Gascoigne before the series starts. There is a quick guide to some early depictions of Christ in Art - as well as some of the various styles of crosses seen over the ages - after the conclusion of the 13 part series. Each episode covers significant events and developments in Christianity, from its origins to modern times. This series describes the experiences and actions of Christians from the birth of Christ, through the Roman Empire, the Renaissance and Lutheran reform to modern times. Episodes: 00 Introduction by Bamber Gasgione 01 A Peculiar People 02 The Christian Empire 03 The Birth of Europe 04 Faith and Fear 05 People of the Book 06 Princes and Prelates 07 Protest and Reform 08 The Conquest of Souls 09 In Search of Tolerance 10 Politeness and Enthusiasm 11 Missions Abroad 12 The Roots of Disbelief 13 The Godless State 14 Bonus: Christ in Art 15 Double Bonus: Name that Cross These videos are freshly derived from DVDs and have Subtitles and Chapter Markers. These videos are all to be used only for free, personal, educational purposes. No other use is authorized. ___________________________________________________________________________________ Episode Descriptions: 00 Introduction by Bamber Gasgione Series host Bamber Gasgione explains what the idea behind the series is … and what they hope you will learn by watching it. 01 A Peculiar People Beginning with the followers of an itinerant preacher in a backwater of the Roman Empire, Christianity became Rome's religion with astonishing speed. 02 The Christian Empire Between the 3rd and 6th centuries CE, Christianity evinced two contrasting impulses that persist to this day: worldliness and asceticism. 03 The Birth of Europe After waves of barbarians swept through the Roman Empire, Charlemagne struck a deal with Pope Leo III to establish both religious and political stability. 04 Faith and Fear Constantly living in the shadow of death, medieval Christians turned to relics to temper God's judgment and built mighty cathedrals to glorify Him. 05 People of the Book Jews, Christians, and Muslims hold sacred not only some of the same scriptures, but also some of the same sites, which they've fought over for centuries. 06 Princes and Prelates Leading up to the Renaissance, as many as three popes simultaneously claimed spiritual and political authority--while dissidents decried their corruption. 07 Protest and Reform Martin Luther's reformist ideas threatened to wreak theological and political havoc, but soon his acolytes had exported Protestant concepts all over Europe. 08 The Conquest of Souls In the 16th century, as Spain expanded Christianity into the New World, the Catholic Church countered the Reformation with its elite \"shock troops,\" the Jesuits. 09 In Search of Tolerance Many Protestants persecuted breakaway sects as vigorously as the Catholic Church has attacked heretics. But when some victims sought refuge in America, they too turned tyrannical. 10 Politeness and Enthusiasm George Whitefield and John Wesley injected emotion into a faith that had become rational, genteel, and--for too many Christians--boring and irrelevant. 11 Missions Abroad By the 19th century, Europe had begun to export its own brand of commerce and Christianity to Africa, while industrialized urban poverty choked religion at home. 12 The Roots of Disbelief It took the Vatican two centuries to accept a heliocentric universe. But science proved only one threat to faith, and change often sparked a fundamentalist reaction. 13 The Godless State? Ironically, Christianity survived--even thrived--in Communist Russia and Poland, while Communism exerted itself in nominally Catholic Italy. 14 ( I found this Bonus Feature while deriving the whole set) Bonus: Christ in Art A slideshow of some early depictions of Christ in Art … 15 ( I found this Double Bonus Feature while deriving the whole set ) Double Bonus: Name that Cross A slideshow of some of the various styles of crosses seen over the ages … This took less than 45 minutes to upload …",
